<p id="p2">Apple added a dedicated Siri mode to the iPhone’s Camera app with iOS 27, which you can read more about in <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-27-review/7/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Federico’s annual review</a>. Now that same feature is set to come to the iPad.</p>
<p id="p3">Earlier this week, Apple released developer beta two for the 27.3 OSes. <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apples-latest-second-27-3-developer-beta-adds-face-unlock-for-iphone-duo-new-mac-privacy-controls-and-more/" rel="noopener noreferrer">I wrote about many of those upcoming changes</a> earlier this week but missed the changes to the iPad’s Camera app. As with iOS, when you open the Camera app in iPadOS 27.3, there will be a dedicated Siri mode. Switch to the new mode and the shutter button becomes a Siri icon. Tap it, and Siri will analyze the scene, describing what it sees. From there, you can use your finger or an Apple Pencil to circle or scrub over individual items, activating Visual Intelligence to search Google, Amazon, and other Visual Intelligence-enabled apps. Here’s a quick demo of it on the latest iPad mini that <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/ipad-mini-2026-review-pushing-the-boundaries-with-apples-smallest-ipad/" rel="noopener noreferrer">I recently reviewed</a>:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/18/siri-camera-mode-ipados-27-3-beta-2-hq-1795029869519-9a885f33a1.gif" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p5">Visual Intelligence is one of those iOS and iPadOS features that I’ve had trouble remembering to use. However, as you can tell from the video, it works really well for things like recognizable products. I’m glad to see this coming to the iPad, especially the mini, which I’m much more likely to use as a camera for capturing my surroundings.</p>
